  {% set simlocalPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Both sell fixed-GB eSIM data at similar prices for small plans, so the real difference isn't the gigabyte rate: it's what each provider bundles around it. AloSIM throws in a free international phone number through Hushed. SimLocal skips the extras and instead offers a genuinely no-questions refund policy, 24/7 WhatsApp support, and even a few physical airport stores.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Similarity: Both Are Pay-Per-GB, Priced Close Together</h2>
  <p>
    Neither AloSIM nor SimLocal sells unlimited-only like <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>. On a small single-country plan the entry prices land close together: AloSIM's US plans start around $4.50 for 1GB, and SimLocal's own site lists global options in roughly the same $4-5 per-GB range. Where they diverge is plan structure: AloSIM prices in single-GB increments, while SimLocal also sells larger bundled plans (its own site shows a UK plan at $13.50 for 20GB, and a US plan at $7.25 for 3GB) that work out cheaper per gigabyte the more data you buy at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Price Snapshot (before any code)</h2>
  <div class="content-box">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Plan</th>
          <th>AloSIM</th>
          <th>SimLocal</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>United States, small plan</td><td>$4.50 (1GB)</td><td>$7.25 (3GB), roughly $2.42/GB</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Larger regional/country bundle</td><td>Priced per GB, no bundled discount tier</td><td>$13.50 (20GB) in the UK, roughly $0.68/GB</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refund policy</td><td>Only if the eSIM fails to activate or is incompatible</td><td>"No Quibble Refunds": any reason, no justification needed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="content-note">AloSIM and SimLocal don't sell identical plan sizes, so this isn't a like-for-like unit price. The pattern that holds either way: SimLocal's bundled plans get cheaper per GB the bigger you buy, while AloSIM's per-GB rate stays flat regardless of plan size.</p>
  </div>

