    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">SimLocal's primary code shown here is ours, so we show it first on that provider's own page; it's not automatically the bigger discount of the two. AloSIM's is a public code, not ours.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is AloSIM or SimLocal the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with AloSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want a free international number bundled in without adding a separate app</li>
        <li>You want the choice of a fixed-GB or unlimited plan from the same checkout</li>
        <li>You're buying a small, single-country plan where AloSIM's flat per-GB rate is already competitive</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with SimLocal if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want a refund option that doesn't hinge on the eSIM technically failing to connect</li>
        <li>You'd rather message support on WhatsApp than dig through an app's help section</li>
        <li>You're buying a bigger data bundle, or you're studying abroad and want a plan built for a longer stay</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>AloSIM's refund guarantee has a timing catch.</strong> It only covers eSIMs that fail to connect or turn out incompatible with your device, not ones you activated and simply didn't use much. SimLocal's "No Quibble Refunds" policy is broader: it doesn't require proving the eSIM was faulty.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Neither provider's support looks like what the badges imply.</strong> AloSIM lists live chat support; SimLocal's 24/7 channel is WhatsApp rather than a website chat widget. If you specifically want to message from your phone mid-trip, SimLocal's approach fits that better even though it's not labeled "live chat" on a browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>AloSIM vs SimLocal FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, AloSIM or SimLocal</h3>
      <p>For a small single-country plan, pricing is close. For a bigger data bundle, SimLocal's larger plans work out cheaper per gigabyte since AloSIM prices in flat single-GB increments with no bundled discount tier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which has the better refund policy</h3>
      <p>SimLocal's is broader: its "No Quibble Refunds" policy doesn't require proving the eSIM was faulty. AloSIM only refunds eSIMs that fail to connect or are incompatible with your device.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either include a phone number</h3>
      <p>AloSIM does, through a partnership with Hushed: a free international number you set up inside the AloSIM app after buying a plan. SimLocal has no equivalent feature.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I buy either eSIM in person, not just online</h3>
      <p>SimLocal runs a small number of physical stores at select airports, on top of its app and website. AloSIM is app-and-browser only.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have plans for a longer stay, like studying abroad</h3>
      <p>SimLocal sells student-specific eSIM plans priced for longer stays. AloSIM's lineup is fixed-GB or unlimited plans without a dedicated long-stay tier.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

